            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "quasi-transient simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "To run over the full 2.58 Myr at a temporal resolution of 1 kyr, calculate comparisons with the proxy data, and produce output files and figures, the emulator ran at several orders of magnitude faster than a standard GCM (minutes, as opposed to months or years). As a comparison, for a GCM such as HadCM3 to carry out the same process (i.e. produce a quasi-transient simulation of 2,580,000 years), even at its optimal speed of approximately 120 model years/day, it would take ~59 years to run.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Quaternary climate emulator",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "arcsin Mielke measure",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "To quantify the model-proxy comparison, we calculate the arcsin Mielke measure, or M score22 to assess the goodness of fit between two variables with the same units, and therefore the skill of the [Quaternary climate] emulator. The resulting skill score has a maximum possible value of 1000, while a score of 0 or negative values indicates no skill22. See Methods, in particular Metric for quantifying model performance.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Quaternary climate emulator",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "root mean squared error (RMSE)",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The performance of the optimised emulators was evaluated, firstly by using a leave-one-out crossvalidation approach. Here, a series of emulators was constructed and used to predict one left out HadCM3 simulation each time. For example, for the interglacial emulator (normally based on 120 HadCM3 simulations), the emulator was built on 119 simulations and used to reproduce the 120th, with the results then compared to the actual 120th HadCM3 simulation. This was then repeated for every possible combination (N=120) of left-out simulations. The number of grid boxes for each experiment calculated to lie within different SD bands, and the root mean squared error (RMSE) averaged across all the emulators were used as a performance indicator.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
